CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) controls how HTML **looks**. It targets elements with **selectors** and applies **declarations**.

### Anatomy of a rule
```css
p {
  color: red;
  font-size: 16px;
}
```
- `p` = selector (which elements)
- `color`, `font-size` = properties
- `red`, `16px` = values

### Three ways to add CSS
```html
<!-- 1. External (best) -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">

<!-- 2. Internal -->
<style>
  p { color: blue; }
</style>

<!-- 3. Inline (avoid) -->
<p style="color: green">Hi</p>
```
External stylesheets keep structure and style separate.

### Element, class, and ID selectors
```css
/* All paragraphs */
p { color: gray; }

/* Everything with class="card" — reusable */
.card { border: 1px solid #ddd; }

/* The one element with id="hero" — unique */
#hero { background: navy; }
```

### Common properties
```css
h1 {
  color: #1f2937;
  font-size: 32px;
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
  text-align: center;
  background-color: #f3f4f6;
  margin: 20px;
  padding: 12px;
}
```

### Specificity
When rules conflict, **specificity** decides: `#id` beats `.class` beats `p`. The last rule wins for equal specificity.
